::GEO_U04_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines sine? {
=A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Sine compares the opposite side to the hypotenuse.
~B. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines cosine.
~C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This defines tangent.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This reverses sine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines cosine? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines sine.
=B. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Cosine compares the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This defines tangent.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This reverses cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio defines tangent? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines sine.
~B. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This defines cosine.
=C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#Tangent compares the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This uses the wrong denominator for tangent.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, opposite = 5 and hypotenuse = 13. What is sin A? {
~A. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#This would use adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This would define tangent if 12 were adjacent.
~C. <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub>#This reverses sine.
=D. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle B, adjacent = 8 and hypotenuse = 17. What is cos B? {
=A. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. <sup>15</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#This would be sine if 15 were opposite.
~C. <sup>15</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This would be tangent with opposite over adjacent.
~D. <sup>17</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This reverses cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle C, opposite = 7 and adjacent = 24. What is tan C? {
~A. <sup>24</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>#This reverses tangent.
=B. <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>24</sub>#Tangent is opposite over adjacent.
~C. <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub>#This would be sine if 25 were the hypotenuse.
~D. <sup>24</sup>&frasl;<sub>25</sub>#This would be cosine if 25 were the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Why must the reference angle be identified? {
~A. The hypotenuse changes with the reference angle.#The hypotenuse is always opposite the right angle.
~B. The triangle stops being right.#The reference angle does not change the triangle type.
=C. Opposite and adjacent depend on the chosen acute angle.#The hypotenuse stays fixed, but opposite and adjacent change with the reference angle.
~D. The right angle becomes acute.#The right angle remains 90&deg;.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio uses the hypotenuse and opposite side? {
~A. Cosine#Cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse.
~B. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~C. Slope#Slope is not one of the three trig ratios here.
=D. Sine#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio uses the adjacent side and hypotenuse? {
=A. Cosine#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. Sine#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~C. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~D. Perimeter#Perimeter is a sum of side lengths, not a trig ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which ratio does not use the hypotenuse? {
~A. Sine#Sine uses the hypotenuse.
=B. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent.
~C. Cosine#Cosine uses the hypotenuse.
~D. Both sine and cosine#Both sine and cosine include the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>SOH means which ratio? {
~A. cos = <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Cosine is represented by CAH.
~B. tan = <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Tangent is represented by TOA.
=C. sin = <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#SOH stands for sine, opposite, hypotenuse.
~D. sin = <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#That is cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>CAH means which ratio? {
~A. sin = <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~B. tan = <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~C. cos = <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This reverses cosine.
=D. cos = <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#CAH stands for cosine, adjacent, hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>TOA means which ratio? {
=A. tan = <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#TOA stands for tangent, opposite, adjacent.
~B. sin = <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~C. cos = <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#Cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse.
~D. tan = <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This reverses tangent.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle D, opposite = 9 and hypotenuse = 41. What is sin D? {
~A. <sup>40</sup>&frasl;<sub>41</sub>#This would be cosine if 40 is adjacent.
=B. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>41</sub>#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>40</sub>#This would be tangent if 40 is adjacent.
~D. <sup>41</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub>#This reverses sine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle E, adjacent = 20 and hypotenuse = 29. What is cos E? {
~A. <sup>21</sup>&frasl;<sub>29</sub>#This would use the opposite side if 21 is opposite.
~B. <sup>21</sup>&frasl;<sub>20</sub>#This would be tangent with opposite over adjacent.
=C. <sup>20</sup>&frasl;<sub>29</sub>#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~D. <sup>29</sup>&frasl;<sub>20</sub>#This reverses cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle F, opposite = 11 and adjacent = 60. What is tan F? {
~A. <sup>60</sup>&frasl;<sub>11</sub>#This reverses tangent.
~B. <sup>11</sup>&frasl;<sub>61</sub>#This would use the hypotenuse and match sine.
~C. <sup>60</sup>&frasl;<sub>61</sub>#This would match cosine if 61 is the hypotenuse.
=D. <sup>11</sup>&frasl;<sub>60</sub>#Tangent is opposite over adjacent.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student writes cos A = opposite over hypotenuse. What correction is needed? {
=A. Use adjacent over hypotenuse for cosine.#Cosine is defined as adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. Use opposite over hypotenuse for cosine.#That repeats the sine ratio.
~C. Use opposite over adjacent for cosine.#That defines tangent.
~D. Use hypotenuse over adjacent for cosine.#This reverses the cosine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student writes tan A = adjacent over opposite. What correction is needed? {
~A. Use adjacent over hypotenuse.#That defines cosine.
=B. Use opposite over adjacent.#Tangent is defined as opposite over adjacent.
~C. Use opposite over hypotenuse.#That defines sine.
~D. Use hypotenuse over opposite.#That is not tangent.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which side is opposite an acute reference angle? {
~A. The side touching the reference angle and not the hypotenuse#That describes the adjacent side.
~B. The side across from the right angle#That describes the hypotenuse.
=C. The side across from the reference angle#Opposite means across from the chosen angle.
~D. The longest leg#Opposite depends on position relative to the reference angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which side is adjacent to an acute reference angle? {
~A. The side across from the angle#That describes the opposite side.
~B. The hypotenuse#The hypotenuse touches acute angles but is not called the adjacent leg in trig ratios.
~C. The side across from the right angle#That is the hypotenuse.
=D. The leg touching the angle#Adjacent is the leg that touches the reference angle.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For angle A, opposite = 6, adjacent = 8, hypotenuse = 10. Which is cos A? {
=A. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub>#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse.
~B. <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub>#This is sine.
~C. <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This is tangent.
~D. <sup>10</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This reverses cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For angle B, opposite = 12, adjacent = 5, hypotenuse = 13. Which is sin B? {
~A. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#This is cosine.
=B. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub>#This is tangent.
~D. <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This reverses sine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For angle C, opposite = 20, adjacent = 21, hypotenuse = 29. Which is tan C? {
~A. <sup>21</sup>&frasl;<sub>20</sub>#This reverses tangent.
~B. <sup>20</sup>&frasl;<sub>29</sub>#This is sine.
=C. <sup>20</sup>&frasl;<sub>21</sub>#Tangent is opposite over adjacent.
~D. <sup>21</sup>&frasl;<sub>29</sub>#This is cosine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which trig ratio should be used when opposite and hypotenuse are known? {
~A. Cosine#Cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse.
~B. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~C. Pythagorean ratio#The requested trig ratio is sine.
=D. Sine#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which trig ratio should be used when adjacent and hypotenuse are known? {
=A. Cosine#Cosine uses adjacent and hypotenuse.
~B. Sine#Sine uses opposite and hypotenuse.
~C. Tangent#Tangent uses opposite and adjacent.
~D. Scale factor#Scale factor is not a right-triangle trig ratio.
}
